title: Hold, or load
mode: draw
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
parts: dff chip:my_mux
needs: my_mux
---- intro
A plain flip-flop takes a new value at **every** tick. A memory that is useful should keep its value until you **ask** it to change. The switch `load` says when:

- `load` is 0: at the tick, the flip-flop takes what it already holds, so nothing changes
- `load` is 1: at the tick, it takes `d`

That is a choice between two inputs, which your **my_mux** makes. One bit of memory like this is one bit of a **register**; a processor's registers are rows of them.
---- task
Make `q` keep its value at each tick, unless `load` is on, when it takes the value of `d`.
---- start
input d 0 0
input load 0 80
clock clk 0 160
led q 440 40
---- reference
input d, load
clock clk
led q
wire t[1]
t = dff((t & ~load) | (d & load), clk)
q = t
---- answer
input d 0 0
input load 0 80
clock clk 0 160
chip m1 my_mux 140 30
dff f1 280 40
led q 440 40
f1.q -> m1.a
d -> m1.b
load -> m1.s
m1.y -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q
---- hint1
The multiplexer's output goes into the flip-flop's `d`. `load` is the multiplexer's `s`.
---- hint2
When `s` is 0 the multiplexer passes `a`, so `a` is what the flip-flop already holds: wire the flip-flop's `q` back into it. `d` goes into `b`.
---- solution
`q` goes back into my_mux's `a`, `d` into `b`, `load` into `s`, and the mux into the flip-flop's `d`. With `load` off, the flip-flop keeps copying itself; with it on, it copies `d`.
---- done
Flip `d` with `load` off and press Step: nothing happens. That is memory you control. Eight of these side by side, sharing `load`, hold a byte.
